      'Pine and fir often have a cone-like shape, sloping branches, and needle leaves. Snow can slide off their branches. Coastal coconut palms tolerate salty coastal conditions and their fruits can float, but they do not simply drink pure seawater.',
      'A cactus stores water in its green stem, which also photosynthesizes. Spines reduce water loss. In waterlogged mangrove soil, breathing roots reach above the surface to obtain air.',
      'Duckweed and water hyacinth are floating plants with air spaces or spongy parts. Lotus and water lilies have roots below the water, long stems, and broad floating leaves. Underwater plants such as tape grass and pondweed take in dissolved gases across their surfaces.',
      'Insectivorous plants still photosynthesize; insects mainly provide extra minerals and nutrients. Venus flytrap leaves close, while pitcher plants have slippery traps and digestive fluid: their lids generally do not snap shut.',
      'Non-green plants such as Indian pipe lack chlorophyll and often get nutrients with help from fungi. Fungi are not plants. The older term saprophyte is often used for decomposers, but non-green plants do not simply eat dead matter directly. Plants also give us food, oils, fibres, wood, paper, rubber, and gum.',
